                    coach_r2_reactive:      `<strong>Spend-to-clear-thresholds is the compliance trap.</strong> You'll get 38% of SKUs back over the line — and so will every competitor, because the regulation forces them to. In 24 months you're where you are today, one class behind, €6M poorer. Where's the advantage, as opposed to the entry ticket?`,
                    coach_r2_productonly:   `<strong>All-in on product: a green appliance made in a brown plant.</strong> Your label jumped, impressive — but 71% of your power is still grid, your unit cost rose, and the carbon-border rule prices your imported components. How long before a cleaner rival undercuts you? Process is the other half of the nexus.`,
                    coach_r2_processonly:   `<strong>All-in on process: you improved the factory and lost the product.</strong> Scope-2 fell and unit cost dropped — genuinely good — but the customer-facing energy label didn't move, the at-risk SKUs still fail, and marketing has nothing visible to sell. Which of these does the customer actually pay for?`,
                    coach_r2_thesisnone:    `<strong>“The threshold is the advantage” isn't a thesis, it's the receipt.</strong> Meeting a rule everyone must meet is the cost of staying in the market, not a reason a customer chooses you. Name the differentiation or the cost edge — or admit you've bought an entry ticket.`,
                    coach_r3_phase_enacted: `<strong>You phased — and the subsidy landed.</strong> Your first stage is in, and the enacted 30% now de-risks the rest: you exercise the option in the money. That's real-options discipline rewarded — you captured the upside without having bet the budget on it.`,
                    coach_r3_phase_noenact: `<strong>You phased — and the subsidy didn't land.</strong> Good. Your first stage stands on its own economics and you simply don't exercise the rest yet. You preserved flexibility instead of stranding capital — exactly what managing a policy as a real option looks like.`,
                    coach_r3_accel_enacted: `<strong>You went all-in and got lucky.</strong> The subsidy landed, so the full tranche pays — but recognise this was a coin-flip you didn't have to take. Had it been delayed or cancelled, two-thirds of the tranche would be underwater. Right outcome, risky process.`,
                    coach_r3_accel_noenact: `<strong>You committed the full tranche — and the subsidy isn't there.</strong> Two-thirds of your process case is suddenly underwater and your unit cost is climbing. Betting a fixed budget on an un-enacted policy isn't strategy, it's hope. Phasing would have protected you.`,
                    coach_r3_hold:          `<strong>Holding everything freezes your emissions progress.</strong> Waiting for certainty feels safe, but you forfeit the cost-and-emissions gains the process tranche was meant to deliver — and if the subsidy was enacted, you've just declined free money. A real option phases; it doesn't freeze.`,
                    coach_r3_hedged:        `<strong>Hedged from the start — that's the discipline.</strong> Because you sized commitments to survive a delayed or cancelled subsidy, the revealed policy state barely dents your plan. You valued flexibility up front instead of needing the policy to break even.`,
                    coach_r3_subsidybet:    `<strong>Your whole case needed the subsidy — and it isn't there.</strong> The break-even rode on public money that didn't arrive, so the process economics collapse and you're carrying the capex alone. This is the textbook subsidy-dependence error: a plan that needs a policy to work isn't a plan.`,
                    coach_r3_carbonabsorb:  `<strong>Absorbing the carbon-border levy into margin is a slow bleed.</strong> +€2.4M/yr straight off EBIT while you change nothing upstream. A cleaner process and re-sourced components would have blunted the same levy instead of paying it every year.`,
                    coach_r3_carbonignore:  `<strong>Ignoring a confirmed carbon-border adjustment is a choice to pay it.</strong> +€2.4M/yr on imported components doesn't disappear because you didn't plan for it — it lands on your cost position and, eventually, your price. Hedge it or wear it.`,
                    coach_r3_premium_thin:  `<strong>A green premium on a thin differentiation edge won't hold.</strong> If your visible advantage isn't real yet, pricing up just hands volume to the leaders. Earn the differentiation first, then price it — premium pricing is a consequence of the edge, not a substitute for it.`,
                    coach_r4_capability:    `<strong>That passes the imitability test.</strong> Integrated product+process know-how, a recycled-material supply lock-in and a proven low-carbon plant are valuable, rare and hard to imitate — the leaders can't replicate that in two years even out-spending you. That's a sustainable advantage, not a head start.`,
                    coach_r4_speed:         `<strong>Speed is a head start with an expiry date.</strong> Getting there first matters only until the leaders, who out-spend you 3-to-1, decide to follow — and regulation will make them. If the only thing protecting your edge is that you went early, name what they hit when they try to copy it.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>“We moved first, that's the advantage” fails the imitability test.</strong> If nothing stops two larger rivals from copying every move within two years, that's not an advantage — it's a lead with a countdown. Sustainable advantage has to be rare <em>and</em> hard to imitate.`,
                    coach_r4_channeldefend: `<strong>Defending the channel is the portfolio-level call.</strong> Offering the partners the new efficient platform makes you the greener supplier they can't easily replace — you protect the 46% of volume instead of optimising the own brand and watching €41M walk to a dual source.`,
                    coach_r4_channelignore: `<strong>Lovely own-brand story — and the 46% just walked.</strong> Both white-label partners put you on notice to dual-source; ignoring them risks €41M of volume to chase a thinner own-brand premium. You optimised 54% and gambled 46%. Was that the bet you meant to make?`,
                    coach_r4_channelexit:   `<strong>Exiting white-label hands €41M and the scale behind it to rivals.</strong> The own-brand line is more profitable but sub-scale — walking from 46% of volume in one move strips the cost base that makes the clean factory pay. A greener platform could have kept them; instead you've shrunk yourself.`,
                    coach_r4_scorecard:     `<strong>The four-KPI scorecard is what a board trusts.</strong> Differentiation, cost, emissions and share together — plus a clear account of what protects the edge — reads as a strategy under control, not a number cherry-picked to flatter. That's how you defend an advantage, not just claim one.`,
                    coach_r4_onekpi:        `<strong>Leading with one flattering KPI is how boards stop trusting you.</strong> They can see all four numbers. Spotlight the best and bury the rest and they'll assume the rest is worse than it is — and start probing exactly where you didn't want them to.`,
                    coach_r4_compliance:    `<strong>“We cleared the thresholds” is the receipt, not the advantage.</strong> So did every rival, because the regulation forced them to. You've defended a compliance cost as if it were a strategy — the board's question, unanswered, is still “where is the edge?”`,

